WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.060
Last year the James Webb Space Telescope&nbsp;
began sending back incredible unprecedented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.160
images of the universe. With its back&nbsp;
to our sun, shielding itself from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.840
saturating light of our star, it turned&nbsp;
its massive golden mirrors to the universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.900
Focusing the light it gathered onto cryogenically&nbsp;
cool sensors, peering back through to the cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:29.400
distant red shifted light emitted 13.5 billion&nbsp;
years ago in the early stages of our universe.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:36.600
An incredible human achievement, but, if we&nbsp;
created a massive radio space telescope. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.640
could peer even further back in time,&nbsp;
detecting signals emitted from hydrogen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.520
atoms in our primordial universe, light that&nbsp;
even the James Webb Telescope can’t detect.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.420
However, radio telescopes are massive, building&nbsp;
a collecting area this large is a challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.340
even on earth. One of our largest radio&nbsp;
telescopes, the Arecibo telescope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:05.760
collapsed in 2020 after 57 years of service&nbsp;
as a result of two supporting cables snapping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.500
This telescope used a natural sinkhole&nbsp;
to shape and support the spherical dish.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.640
Radar waves from this distant past&nbsp;
are blocked by our atmosphere,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.460
but what if we could build&nbsp;
something like this in space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.800
using naturally formed craters in the&nbsp;
same way the Arecibo used its sinkhole.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.860
That is precisely the plan proposed by&nbsp;
NASA scientists. A radio telescope on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.180
far side of the moon, using one of the&nbsp;
many craters pock marking its surface.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.100
It would be the largest space telescope ever&nbsp;
constructed, and using clever engineering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.020
it could be VASTLY cheaper than the&nbsp;
James Webb Telescope. AThis is its story.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:54.960
The basic concept of a massive radio telescope&nbsp;
is not new. The arecibo observatory in Puerto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.120
Rico was a huge 300 metre diameter&nbsp;
dish. But this moon telescope would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.460
be even bigger. With a 1.3 kilometre wide&nbsp;
crater the engineers of this project will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.920
be able to suspend a dish with a diameter&nbsp;
50 metres greater than arecibo observatory.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.960
This proposal is part of a program at NASA&nbsp;
where researchers are encouraged to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.820
about futuristic technologies that could be&nbsp;
plausible within the next 10 to 20 years.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.880
So how plausible is this? Building&nbsp;
structures on the moon is not easy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.740
The core idea behind this telescope is&nbsp;
to use a crater on the moon as a bowl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.180
removing the need for heavy support&nbsp;
structures, and constructing the dish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.460
from wire meshes that sag naturally under&nbsp;
the moon's gravity to form a reflective dish.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.080
The use of the mesh wire drastically&nbsp;
reduces the weight of the system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.080
while still allowing radio waves to bounce off it.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.100
As long as the gaps in the mesh&nbsp;
are shorter than the incoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.680
signal’s wavelength, the wave will bounce off.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.420
Just like the holes in the screen mesh of&nbsp;
your microwave oven allow visible light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.860
to pass through but reflect the longer&nbsp;
wavelengths of the microwaves themselves.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:10.020
In order for this to work, we need to&nbsp;
find the perfect crater, and scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.240
have been scouring over data obtained from&nbsp;
the lunar reconnaissance orbiter since 2009.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:21.000
First it needs to be on the far side of the&nbsp;
moon. The moon is tidally locked to the Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.940
and thus the far side is always facing away&nbsp;
from earth, and so it shields it from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:32.460
radio noise we emit. The further away the crater&nbsp;
is from the near side of the moon the better.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:38.100
To fit the 350 metre wide dish the crater&nbsp;
needs to be 5 kilometres in circumference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:46.260
and 175 metres deep. It needs to be as smooth as&nbsp;
possible, lacking any large boulders or mounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:52.800
The rim must also be as level as possible to&nbsp;
allow for easy anchoring of the hanging wires.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.700
Finally, we want to point away from&nbsp;
the centre of the milky way galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.340
our galaxy. To observe the radio waves&nbsp;
emitted from the early universe we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.220
to point our telescope to the&nbsp;
quieter parts of the universe.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:12.000
But we can’t point a crater. We have to rely on&nbsp;
the moon's natural motion to sweep a section of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.220
the sky. This traced sweep changes depending&nbsp;
on the north/ south location of the crater.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.420
By choosing a crater further&nbsp;
north, between 10 and 20 degrees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.780
the telescope can avoid the&nbsp;
noise from the Milky Way galaxy.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:32.520
These criteria narrow down the options from 82,000&nbsp;
craters on the far side of the moon, to just 300.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:38.400
From those 300, 50 were chosen for more&nbsp;
careful consideration. This crater here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.100
almost 10 degrees north, was the chosen one.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.480
Construction on the moon is perhaps the&nbsp;
largest challenge facing this project.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.360
The telescope is going to focus on capturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:54.240
information from the electromagnetic&nbsp;
spectrum between 4.7 MHz and 47 MHz.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:05:01.020
This corresponds to a wavelength between 6.4&nbsp;
to 64 metres. This leads to one of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:06.000
engineering design constraints. The dish&nbsp;
needs to reflect and focus these waves.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:11.520
On Earth, mass is not a huge constraint&nbsp;
for a stationary structure. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.260
we will need to ship these materials from&nbsp;
earth at a huge expense per kilogram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.200
so we want to minimise weight where possible.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:24.300
We can form a support structure with&nbsp;
lightweight carbon fibre cables. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.260
material also helps with maintaining the&nbsp;
structure's shape as the temperatures

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.460
on the moon swings wildly&nbsp;
from -170 degrees celsius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.500
to 120 degrees celsius over&nbsp;
its night and day time cycles.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.960
A temperature swing that would cause aluminium&nbsp;
to expand and contract, warping the shape.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.860
These carbon fibre wires will then&nbsp;
be anchored on the crater sides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.520
with the natural sag in the wire&nbsp;
forming the support structure.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.540
However, hanging wires will&nbsp;
naturally create a catenary shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.180
which is not suitable as a concave mirror.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:02.160
We need to create a shape capable of&nbsp;
focusing the reflected electromagnetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.940
waves to point inside the crater,&nbsp;
where we can hang a receiver.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.800
To get a better-focused beam the&nbsp;
shape must be closer to a half circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.360
but how can we achieve that with a&nbsp;
wire that has just two anchor points?

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.940
This is where things get interesting. Wires&nbsp;
have no compressive strength and must hang in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:26.520
pure tension. Hang a wire from a single point and&nbsp;
it will hang straight down, hang it between two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.100
points and it will sag downwards, until once&nbsp;
again the entire string is in tension. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:37.920
we can put more weight, it will sag with a deeper&nbsp;
parabolic arch to allow it to remain in tension.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.180
We can take advantage of this by&nbsp;
hanging weight strategically along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:47.580
its length to alter its shape. This can be done&nbsp;
by tailoring the wire's shape along its length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:51.660
or by coating the wire in materials of&nbsp;
different densities along its length.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:56.760
But anchoring the tethers to the rim&nbsp;
is easier said than done, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:00.120
if we are going to be constructing&nbsp;
it without the help of astronauts.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:07.740
Thankfully the moon's weaker gravity makes things&nbsp;
easier. The moon's gravity is about 16% Earths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:14.700
Supporting a 2000 kg telescope on the moon is&nbsp;
equivalent to holding up just 320 kg on Earth.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:21.000
The question is, how do we deploy them.&nbsp;
One plan involves using duaxel robots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.800
currently being developed by JPL to&nbsp;
manually tow the wires into place.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:32.340
Another plan is to form the anchoring wire shape&nbsp;
above the crater before landing, either by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:37.980
lander firing anchors before landing, or through&nbsp;
multiple landers landing at their anchor sites.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:43.260
However, the cheapest option involves&nbsp;
a single lunar lander that will land in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:47.580
the centre of the crater and fire the&nbsp;
anchors above the crater rim. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:52.680
anchors are similar to boat anchors, and upon&nbsp;
tensioning will dig into the lunar regolith.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.760
However, the lunar regolith is&nbsp;
razor sharp and great caution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.660
needs to be taken to minimise friction&nbsp;
between the tethers and the ground.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:04.920
Depending on projectiles also&nbsp;
requires extensive testing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:09.300
validation to ensure their reliability&nbsp;
and prevent any potential failures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:14.580
a difficult thing to simulate, which is&nbsp;
highly dependent on the terrain of the site.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:21.300
On the other hand, relying only on projectiles&nbsp;
greatly decreases cost and complexity with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:27.420
estimated total cost of 2.4 billion dollars.&nbsp;
It’s estimated that deploying the wires with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:33.600
rovers would cost 4.5 billion. For comparison&nbsp;
the James Webb Telescope cost 10 billion=.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.960
Once deployed, these anchors are the skeleton&nbsp;
upon which the rest of the structure is built.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:44.100
The antenna receiver is raised to the&nbsp;
focal point of the structure first.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.460
If deployed by individual robotic anchor points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:51.480
the tensioning of these receiver wires&nbsp;
could be done from the anchor points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.980
but this again would pull the wires&nbsp;
along extremely abrasive lunar regolith.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:01.440
Ideally a robot pulley system can climb&nbsp;
the existing anchoring wires and deploy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.100
these structures without causing&nbsp;
any more friction on the crater rim.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:09.120
Next is the reflector mesh,&nbsp;
this mesh will be made from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:12.579
ultra-light reflective material&nbsp;
like a gold plated molybdenum.

00:09:12.579 --> 00:09:14.580
(use their animation)
To deploy the reflector, guide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:20.760
wires are pulled to the top of the anchor wires,&nbsp;
which in turn will unfurl the wire mesh, carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:26.520
stowed aboard, inspired by origami. This concept&nbsp;
has been modelled in multiple space missions.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:33.180
From solar arrays to inflatable heat shields. With&nbsp;
good reason, origami, with its intricate folds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:38.700
allows engineers to pack large structures&nbsp;
into extremely tight spaces. The perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:44.520
solution for packing a 350 metre wide radar&nbsp;
dish into a space confined lunar lander.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:50.160
Once deployed the radio telescope can&nbsp;
begin sending data back to earth. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.020
telescope won’t give us pretty images like&nbsp;
the James Webb Telescope. It will instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:59.880
give us data on how hydrogen is distributed&nbsp;
in the universe, but how is that useful?

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:06.300
It will give us a new understanding of how the&nbsp;
universe formed. Right now the main hypothesis is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:12.420
that hydrogen distribution should be uniform over&nbsp;
space and time. But that assumption of uniformity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.980
was also made about cosmic background&nbsp;
radiation, which turned out to be false.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:21.300
Assumptions like these need to be&nbsp;
tested and not just given as a fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:26.160
and this telescope will be able to&nbsp;
detect these hydrogens, but how?

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:30.540
In the early universe, before stars&nbsp;
began to form, there was no light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:34.800
but there were hydrogen atoms,&nbsp;
a lot of hydrogen atoms. Cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.840
interstellar, hydrogens are at their lowest&nbsp;
possible energy level, and radiate nothing.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:45.120
However, on occasion, these atoms can&nbsp;
become excited to a higher energy state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.060
through collisions with other atomic particles.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:52.440
At some point, through natural decay,&nbsp;
or through a secondary collision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:58.380
this energy needs to be radiated out again.&nbsp;
And the energy is released with a photon of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:05.460
very specific wavelength. 21 cm long. Giving&nbsp;
the phenomenon the name of the 21 cm line.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:10.200
By listening for radio waves at this&nbsp;
particular frequency, we can quantify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:15.240
hydrogen formations across the universe. But&nbsp;
the goal is more than just collecting signals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:19.800
from one point in time, the goal would&nbsp;
be to track these formations over time.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.660
We can do this because of redshifting.&nbsp;
As the universe expands, it stretches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:29.280
the frequencies to lower and lower&nbsp;
frequencies. We can identify the age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:33.480
of the signal by how much the signal&nbsp;
has shifted into longer wavelengths.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:39.720
This data is crucial for refining our models&nbsp;
regarding the conditions that led to the formation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:45.180
of stars and providing insights into how the&nbsp;
universe gradually cooled following the big bang.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:50.160
Of course, the telescope needs to send&nbsp;
data back to Earth. This is where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.780
telescope could potentially benefit from&nbsp;
other lunar missions. When the Apollo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:58.740
astronauts ventured to the far side of the&nbsp;
moon, they could not communicate with earth.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:04.620
The situation has improved marginally&nbsp;
since. China launched the first and only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:08.460
communication relay satellite for&nbsp;
the far side of the moon in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:14.760
which parked in the Earth-Moon L2 lagrange point,&nbsp;
and is communicating with their lunar lander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:18.660
which became the first lunar lander to&nbsp;
soft land on the far side of the moon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:25.440
in 2019. NASA and the ESA will need their own&nbsp;
communication relays for this mission to operate.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:32.100
Both NASA and the ESA have allocated substantial&nbsp;
resources for lunar exploration. In particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:36.420
NASA's Artemis program, aiming to&nbsp;
send humans to the moon's south pole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:39.120
is set to be in full swing in the coming decade.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:41.700
With the increasing number of lunar missions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:46.080
communication between the moon and Earth&nbsp;
will become more accessible and efficient.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:50.880
The hydrogen mapping mission itself is&nbsp;
only scheduled for 1 year of operation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.760
The team is confident that they can gather enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:57.720
data in that time to piece together&nbsp;
the evolution of the early universe.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:03.000
But of course, the massive radio telescope&nbsp;
is capable of much more than just detecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:08.460
early hydrogen signals. The long radio wave&nbsp;
spectrum is full of other useful information.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:13.320
When radio waves travel through magnetic&nbsp;
fields they are split and altered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:18.360
and radio telescopes can pick up on this to&nbsp;
detect and quantify the magnetic fields of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:24.000
exoplanets. Potentially identify exoplanets&nbsp;
life could survive on. The team has already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:29.160
mapped the known exoplanet systems that&nbsp;
traverse the field of view of the telescope.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:34.080
This is an exciting project with a lot of&nbsp;
scientific potential. How could we not get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.820
excited by the potential of a massive telescope&nbsp;
being constructed on the far side of the moon?

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:45.120
All projects like this begin at the same&nbsp;
point, before anything is manufactured the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:50.520
entire design will be prototyped in a computer&nbsp;
aided design system, or as we call in engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:56.220
CAD. Before I started this YouTube channel&nbsp;
I spent days every week designing parts in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:59.580
CAD software and even today I still mess&nbsp;
around with it for 3D printing projects.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:03.480
The software I use for that is&nbsp;
OnShape. OnShape is available to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:08.220
try for free with the link in the description.
